00:02:15.313 --> 00:02:16.480
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Excellent.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.590
So we're going to start with
a question about software use.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.240
It's 2021, and
whatever you're doing,

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.710
you're always using software.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.822
Now, Rebecca how does Go
provide dependency management?

00:02:30.822 --> 00:02:32.530
REBECCA STAMBLER: So
Go code is typically

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.960
grouped into packages,
and then related packages

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.928
can be grouped into
a larger module.

00:02:36.928 --> 00:02:38.470
And so a module is
basically the unit

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:40.480
of dependency management in Go.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.810
What happens is you
can import libraries

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.310
that are provided by different
modules into your own module.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.310
And the way that you do that
is by having a go.mod file

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:49.810
at the root of your project.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.300
And that go.mod
file will specify

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.820
each module that you
require and its version

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:55.760
that you require that.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.610
So that way, you'll
get reproducible builds

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.840
in your projects.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.200
And actually, it's really simple
to get started with Go modules.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.030
All you really need to
do is run a command,

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.175
just go mod init
inside of a directory,

00:03:06.175 --> 00:03:08.320
and it will create this
go.mod file for you.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.240
And then, the go
command will allow

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.040
you to download, and
upgrade, and downgrade

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.630
your different dependencies.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.660
And also, you can also get
this feature for your editor,

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.612
so you can manage your
modules through your editor,

00:03:22.612 --> 00:03:24.070
and you don't ever
have to actually

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.800
manually edit that go.mod file.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.450
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Excellent.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:31.060
You mentioned reproducible
results, and that is definitely

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.470
something that is dear to the
heart of the security team,

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:37.420
because that means
that even if something

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:40.840
changes in the outside
world, a build of a Go module

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:42.940
will always be the
same, independent,

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.670
and there's no way for
external compromises

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:50.590
to influence the
application after its Go has

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:52.030
become reproducible.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:56.110
Now, we talked about how
the modules are managed,

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:59.650
but the next question,
I think, is for Julie.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.600
How do I choose
dependencies for my project?

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.600
JULIE QIU: Yeah, that's
a really great question.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:08.710
And I mentioned that my team
works primarily on the Go

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:10.362
package discovery site.

00:04:10.362 --> 00:04:12.070
And it was actually
built for the purpose

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:15.400
of helping developers choose
their third party dependencies.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.959
So as we were
building the site, I

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.899
would say that we thought about
surfing [INAUDIBLE] information

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:22.700
that belonged to
three main categories.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.660
And I would break these down
into popularity, quality,

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.160
and stability.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.170
So a little bit about each one.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.990
Popularity, I think, is
important because it really

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.540
just acts as a social signal.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.333
It tells you other people
are using this package.

00:04:37.333 --> 00:04:39.250
They've probably already
done the work for you

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:42.500
to evaluate whether or not
this package is dependable.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.650
And if the author of
the package decides

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.630
that they want to stop
maintaining the project,

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:48.580
it's likely that one
of the many people who

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:50.920
are depending on this project
might step up and continue

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.900
to maintain it.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.730
When I say "quality,"
quality really

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:58.120
just means looking at the
quality of the code itself.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.490
Is this project well-documented?

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.140
Is it well-tested?

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.870
And does it generally just
look like quality Go code

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:08.350
that you feel comfortable
including in your own project?

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:09.907
And I think lastly, stability.

00:05:09.907 --> 00:05:12.490
That's the topic that's probably
the most dear to the security

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:13.780
team's heart.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.960
It means figuring out whether
or not this package is reliable.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:19.960
So security vulnerabilities
is definitely

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.370
one of those features.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:22.960
You want to look at
a package and see,

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:25.145
does it have a history of
security vulnerabilities?

00:05:25.145 --> 00:05:27.520
But in general, you're just
really looking at the package

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.040
to see, is this something
that I can not only

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.190
depend on today, but months
from now or years from now,

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:37.030
and looking at conventions, like
Rebecca mentioned, with Go Mod

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:39.580
Tools, whether or not the author
follows semantic versioning,

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:40.810
those are all some
things you want to look

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:42.190
at when considering stability.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:46.270
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Excellent.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:48.640
Now, the next
question, I feel like,

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.510
is definitely for me and Katie.

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:54.880
What are some new things
coming to Go that will

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.360
help with end-to-end security?

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.630
One thing that I want
to mention myself

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.310
is that we are building a
solution for vulnerability

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.240
patching in Go.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.250
So what we want to handle
is when there are already

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:13.870
vulnerabilities that are
known in the outside world,

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:16.060
we want to make
sure that you can

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:18.790
get to know when those
vulnerabilities affect

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:21.410
your application or any
of its dependencies.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:22.910
So there are two pieces to this.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:24.460
One is that we're
going to maintain

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:27.700
a first-party vulnerability
database, where we track

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.410
and collect all
vulnerabilities that

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:32.950
have been disclosed publicly
in any Go module that's

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:34.330
publicly importable.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:39.190
And then, we'll provide tooling,
tentatively called a Go audit,

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:42.430
that will use the database,
access the database,

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:45.820
and then report which
of the vulnerabilities

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:48.623
that are known affect
your application.

00:06:48.623 --> 00:06:50.290
What's interesting
about it is that it's

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:53.080
going to use static analysis
to make sure that it only

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:54.700
shows you the ones
that are actually

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.270
relevant to your application.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:59.080
So even if you do depend on a
version of a dependency that

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:02.357
has a vulnerability, if there
is no way for your application

00:07:02.357 --> 00:07:04.690
to reach that code-- maybe
because you're not using that

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.690
mode, or plugin, or setting--

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.520
the code, it will not
tell you about it,

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.020
so that when you
get a report, you

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:13.000
know that you can
prioritize fixing it

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:18.060
and spend the time to figure
out what it is and why.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:20.538
And Katie, do you want
to talk about fuzzing?

00:07:20.538 --> 00:07:22.080
KATIE HOCKMAN: Yeah,
I would love to.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.570
Fuzzing is something that's
very near and dear to my heart.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.990
I'm really excited about
this coming really soon.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:30.740
Fuzzing is-- like
I said before, it's

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:33.295
a kind of automated
testing that's

00:07:33.295 --> 00:07:34.920
a little bit different
from unit tests,

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.870
and it's something
that, in the past,

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:38.370
has often really
just been leveraged

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:39.960
by security researchers.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:41.670
And while that's
really great, we

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:44.730
think that every developer
who wants to use this tool

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:46.470
can benefit from it.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:49.650
It can help find bugs that
your unit tests might miss.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:53.700
And how it can do that is
in its kind of fuzzing mode,

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.280
it actually can run
a fuzzing engine,

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.400
which generates inputs for you.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.020
It can do some
really smart things

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:04.680
to try to find new coverage
and find new crashes.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:06.750
And in the default
mode, it's going

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:10.170
to work a lot like a
unit test will, where

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:13.650
you can provide the inputs, and
you can provide what you think

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.660
it should do, and
then it will report

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.460
to you if there's a problem.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:19.830
And there are existing
tools out there right now

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.080
for fuzzing in Go,
but none that are

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.172
built into the standard
distribution or part of the Go

00:08:25.172 --> 00:08:26.020
toolchain.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.437
And that's something
that we think

00:08:27.437 --> 00:08:30.570
is going to be really important
to help just the general user

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:34.380
experience for Go developers
and provide a first class

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.090
service with fuzzing.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:38.260
So I'm really
excited about this,

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:40.289
and I'd love to answer
more questions about it.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:42.780
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Yeah, I'm
also very excited about it,

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.420
because, indeed, I used
fuzzing as a security

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:48.600
researcher in the
past, but it always

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:52.470
felt weird that fuzzing is
considered this thing that you

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.020
prepare once, you write the
fuzzer, you run it once,

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.090
you find your bug
bounties or not,

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:58.485
and then you throw it away.

00:08:58.485 --> 00:08:58.980
KATIE HOCKMAN: Yeah.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:08:59.580
FILIPPO VALSORDA:
Would you imagine

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.830
if unit tests were
something that somebody just

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:03.820
writes once to find
bugs in your program

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:07.860
and then throws them away
after reporting the bugs?

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.210
So we do have a question
from Alan about fuzzing.

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:15.330
How long do you think it will
be before the new Go fuzzing

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.420
proposal uncovers
bugs in hardware?

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:21.600
KATIE HOCKMAN: I love that
question, so thanks, Alan,

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.092
for asking.

00:09:23.092 --> 00:09:24.300
I think it's going to depend.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:28.230
I mean, in my
optimistic view, I am

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.820
hoping that people can start
using this tool really,

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:31.680
really soon.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.200
We're kind of on the cusp
of being ready for people

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:37.050
to start beta testing it
in our development branch,

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:38.970
so people should
definitely be looking out

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:41.845
for upcoming
announcements like that.

00:09:41.845 --> 00:09:44.470
I'll definitely be posting it on
Twitter so you can watch that.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.453
But at that point,
as soon as people

00:09:46.453 --> 00:09:48.120
are ready to start
writing fuzz targets,

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:49.530
they definitely should.

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:51.908
And depending on how good
the fuzzing engine is--

00:09:51.908 --> 00:09:53.700
which is what we're
going to be, hopefully,

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.430
finding out during the
beta testing phase--

00:09:56.430 --> 00:10:00.090
the hope is that it can
find bugs pretty quickly.

00:10:00.090 --> 00:10:01.830
What we have right
now is already

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:05.010
using what's called
coverage guidance, meaning

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.620
that it can actually
see if there's new code

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:09.630
coverage during
the testing process

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:12.910
and determine if that's an
interesting input or not.

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:17.010
And if it is, then it can focus
on those inputs going forward

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:19.545
and get smarter as it runs.

00:10:19.545 --> 00:10:22.890
And so the hope is that as
soon as people start using it,

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:27.180
they're going to be
able to find these bugs.

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:28.500
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Excellent.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:32.100
The next question, from
Jamie, is about something

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.710
along the lines of sandboxing.

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:38.110
Jamie asks, are there any plans
to limit dependencies can be?

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:40.590
For example, declaring
that a dependency package

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.620
can make network calls or
institute child processes?

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:46.650
It's something that's extremely
interesting to think about,

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:49.200
and it's something that I always
felt like JavaScript could

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.910
really do, because
JavaScript already

00:10:50.910 --> 00:10:54.540
has a full-fledged
sandboxing story.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:57.600
Or at least it did until
Spectre happened and realized

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.340
that there isn't
really a security

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:03.340
boundary between things that
run inside the same process.

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:08.700
So really, I don't think we
have any plans for Go for that.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.740
Because remember that
in Go, you can do things

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:14.640
like just use unsafe and
access memory directly.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:17.340
And although there used
to be a safe mode, where

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:20.370
the compiler would try
to disallow things that

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:22.140
look a little too--
that would allow you

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:26.730
to mess with other
dependencies, we never really

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.492
trusted the safety
of that, and it's

00:11:28.492 --> 00:11:30.075
been removed, if I
remember correctly,

00:11:30.075 --> 00:11:33.640
in Go 1.12 or
something like that.

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:35.340
Our recommendation
is very much more

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:38.400
to use instead
process sandboxing.

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:42.300
For example, the Go playground
uses Gvisor to just sandbox

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:46.410
the entire process
and not smaller

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:48.000
units within the process.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:51.120
Again, Spectre is
really the problem

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:56.130
with that kind of within-process
sandboxing, and too long

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:57.787
to go into details,
but Spectre is

00:11:57.787 --> 00:12:00.300
a vulnerability in
the CPUs themselves

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:03.913
and truly a whole
topic of itself.

00:12:03.913 --> 00:12:06.330
I will start talking about
Spectre, because I can probably

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:09.266
just go on talking about
Spectre, as Katie knows.

00:12:09.266 --> 00:12:15.660
But instead, I'll take the
next question from Guillaume

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:17.190
hopefully.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:21.690
So how to be sure that the
public Go library that I use

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:25.160
is secure and doesn't contain
backdoors or something else?

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:29.160
I feel like this is something
up your alley, Julie.

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:31.268
JULIE QIU: Yeah, that's
a really great question.

00:12:31.268 --> 00:12:33.060
And I think I mentioned
stability is really

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:34.685
one of the things
that you want to look

00:12:34.685 --> 00:12:37.200
for when you're thinking
about a third party package.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:40.320
So today, I would say that
probably the best way to do it

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.580
is actually just to treat the
code like it's your own Go

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:44.080
code.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:46.770
So when you pull the code
down, run the tests that

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:48.490
are part of the repository.

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:49.870
Hopefully, there
are tests there.

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.910
If not, that's a pretty
strong signal for you.

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.490
And maybe even
write your own tests

00:12:53.490 --> 00:12:57.240
to see if you can uncover things
that you're hoping to look for.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.080
But on our end, for
the Go team, one

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:01.080
of the things I'm really
excited about this year

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:03.090
is that on the package
discovery site,

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:05.443
we're building out a
version history timeline

00:13:05.443 --> 00:13:07.110
so that when you're
looking at packages,

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:10.830
you can look at what the changes
are from version to version

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:12.250
for a package.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:15.380
So for example, is the
author deleting identifiers

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:16.380
from version to version?

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:18.705
That's probably a
bad stability signal.

00:13:18.705 --> 00:13:20.580
And then, as Filippo
also actually mentioned,

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:22.788
that the security team is
working on a Go [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:22.788 --> 00:13:23.790
billing database.

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:25.165
And so the package
discovery site

00:13:25.165 --> 00:13:27.373
is going to be one of the
places where we can surface

00:13:27.373 --> 00:13:28.690
that information for you.

00:13:28.690 --> 00:13:30.870
And so when you go
and look at a package

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.000
and it has a security
vulnerability at the most

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.670
recent version, there will be
a little piece of information

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.982
there to say, just
so you know, this

00:13:37.982 --> 00:13:39.190
has a security vulnerability.

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:41.110
Be careful when you're
using this package.

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:43.027
So I'm excited for that
to come out this year.

00:13:43.027 --> 00:13:44.568
FILIPPO VALSORDA:
I also feel like it

00:13:44.568 --> 00:13:47.400
goes back to what you were
talking about with social proof

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.500
and popularity.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.870
Because really,
using software always

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:55.620
involves a trust relationship.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:59.220
We try to do our best to make
tools to assess the trust

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:02.310
relationship and to minimize
it, but the trust relationship

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:03.990
is fundamentally there.

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:08.370
And something that I think makes
the Go ecosystem much better

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:12.030
suited than others to handle
the trust relationship

00:14:12.030 --> 00:14:13.800
is that there's a
culture of limiting

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:16.100
how many dependencies you have.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:20.790
So there isn't a silver
bullet to make sure

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:23.460
that there aren't backdoors
in your dependency,

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:25.750
but the fewer
dependencies you have,

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:28.540
the fewer liabilities you have.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:32.880
Now, we have another question
about fuzzing from Stephen.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:34.950
Will there be a
way to seed fuzzing

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:39.160
or trace the seed for a fuzz
so that errors can be repeated?

00:14:39.160 --> 00:14:43.770
So how will I know how to
repeat the failing tests?

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:46.142
KATIE HOCKMAN: Yeah,
so there's two ways

00:14:46.142 --> 00:14:48.600
to interpret that question, so
I guess I could answer both.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:52.830
As far as knowing
whether or not--

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:56.760
so I guess the first part of the
question is, if, in this flow,

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:00.810
fuzzing generates an input
that causes a crasher,

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:04.170
how do we know that that
crasher is actually fixed,

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:07.530
and how can we run that
crasher again in the future

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:10.500
and check it into our
repository as a unit test?

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:13.380
What's really cool
about this UX,

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:17.190
basically, was a very
intentional choice

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:19.350
to try to do this by
default for the user.

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:22.320
So if you were to
run go test -fuzz

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:25.260
and then specify your
target, and it'll run.

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:28.330
And then, if it finds a crash,
it'll actually print out hey,

00:15:28.330 --> 00:15:29.310
we found a crash.

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:31.470
We wrote it to
test data for you,

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:32.880
and here's the name of the file.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:36.090
And to run it again,
that specific C

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:39.150
corpus in the [INAUDIBLE],,
you could actually just

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:44.640
run go test -run fuzz
whatever in that file,

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:47.490
or you can just run go test,
and they'll just include

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:48.660
that as a normal unit test.

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:51.150
So basically, it
can go from fuzzing

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:52.860
to just being a unit
test without you

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:55.210
needing to do anything at all.

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:58.750
And I think that that's a
really important part of the UX.

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:00.500
And if you were to
interpret the question,

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:03.067
instead of actually running it
the same way every single time

00:16:03.067 --> 00:16:04.650
for debugging, there
is actually a way

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:07.440
to do that, too, that we're
looking into where you actually

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:08.825
provide the seed for randomness.

00:16:08.825 --> 00:16:10.200
But that's probably
not something

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:11.970
that most people
would benefit from.

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:14.580
That's more just for debugging
the actual fuzzing engine.

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:16.247
FILIPPO VALSORDA: I
played with the flow

00:16:16.247 --> 00:16:19.350
that Katie is describing,
and I was extremely happy,

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:21.630
because the fuzzer
finds something,

00:16:21.630 --> 00:16:23.580
and usually, we
go to fuzzers, we

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:25.950
have to then write
more code to try

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:29.640
to run the fuzzer input into
your program to figure out

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:30.540
how that happened.

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:32.670
As you said, with this,
you just run more tests,

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:33.570
and it fails again.

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:35.737
And then you, try fixing
it, and you run more tests,

00:16:35.737 --> 00:16:37.982
and it fails again--
let's be honest.

00:16:37.982 --> 00:16:39.690
And then you keep
doing this a few times,

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:43.740
and eventually, the test
passes, and you know that works,

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:47.760
and you did not write one
more line of filler code

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:49.170
or boilerplate.

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:51.480
So yes, I'm extremely
excited for this.

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:53.430
Now, a question from Peter.

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:56.640
Rebecca, I think this one is
definitely for you, and is,

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.370
what is the best IDE for
secure code development?

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:01.830
If you like more than
what's the best one,

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:03.760
we can talk about what
they have in common,

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:06.540
too, because I know that there
isn't that much difference

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.611
in what runs underneath, right?

00:17:08.611 --> 00:17:09.569
REBECCA STAMBLER: Yeah.

00:17:09.569 --> 00:17:11.970
So basically, the two
most popular editors

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:15.920
I'd use for Go development
are VSCode and GoLand.

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:18.950
And GoLand is a JetBrains
IDE, so I won't really

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:20.030
be able to speak to that.

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:22.634
But I can speak to VSCode,
and Vim, and Emacs,

00:17:22.634 --> 00:17:26.480
and all the other editors
that the Go team does support.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.069
And so as I
mentioned earlier, we

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:30.170
have gopls, which
is the back end that

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:32.780
provides IDE-like features
for all of these editors.

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:35.280
And it has all the same
features across the board.

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.900
And so linters that are
available through gopls

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:41.640
are available in VS
and all the same.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.810
But since VSCode is the
most popular editor,

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:48.920
the Go team also supports the
VSCode plugin for Go directly.

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:50.900
And it does have a few
additional features

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:54.470
that community members built
in before the Go team took

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:57.290
ownership of it, so it has a
little bit more linters that

00:17:57.290 --> 00:18:00.230
are just built in by default.
Things like GoLang [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:03.530
are available directly
in VSCode Go plugin.

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:07.100
So that can be useful
and help with security,

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:10.502
but in general, VSCode
has a few features

00:18:10.502 --> 00:18:12.710
that are really nice for
security as well as as that.

00:18:12.710 --> 00:18:14.090
Right now, they're doing
something called Secure--

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:15.950
or, Trusted Workspaces,
which basically

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:18.470
means that you can indicate
that you trust a workspace.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:20.928
And that will allow you to run
arbitrary commands in there.

00:18:20.928 --> 00:18:23.340
And we've just been, in
general, about a lot of the Go

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:23.850
extensions.

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:25.475
They run the go
command under the hood.

00:18:25.475 --> 00:18:27.110
And so we've been
a bit more careful

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:30.650
lately about making sure that
we check that the workspace is

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:32.193
trusted before we do that.

00:18:32.193 --> 00:18:34.610
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Anybody
paying attention to the security

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:36.642
updates might have noticed
that that's actually

00:18:36.642 --> 00:18:37.850
something that's fairly hard.

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:41.300
In Go, we have
this security model

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:45.110
where we consider looking at
the code and building code,

00:18:45.110 --> 00:18:46.760
not the same as running it.

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:49.400
Other ecosystems just
don't have the distinction,

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:51.320
and that's a design decision.

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:54.530
But in Go, we guarantee that
downloading a dependency,

00:18:54.530 --> 00:18:58.070
building it, and
opening it in VSCode Go

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:00.200
is not going to
execute code from it.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.180
And that's something
that we really

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:04.490
like for triaging
vulnerable code,

00:19:04.490 --> 00:19:07.250
because [INAUDIBLE] we can open
random code from the internet

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:10.680
without being worried about that
code trying to run something

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:12.860
in the background on us.

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:16.850
Now, we have another question
that we got before the session

00:19:16.850 --> 00:19:20.450
that I feel like
dovetails well on that.

00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:23.600
Does Go have a debugger, and
what are upcoming new features

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:24.183
for debugging?

00:19:24.183 --> 00:19:25.642
REBECCA STAMBLER:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:19:25.642 --> 00:19:27.527
So Go has a debugger
called Delve,

00:19:27.527 --> 00:19:30.110
and it's had it for a long time,
though I know a lot of people

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:32.000
often don't actually--

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:34.040
they may not be aware
that Go has a debugger.

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.140
But you can access
it through any of--

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:38.450
there are plug-ins, basically,
for all these editors,

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:42.200
like VSCode and et cetera.

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:44.640
But there's a lot of new
stuff coming in this area.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:48.860
So actually, the way that
gopls works, the IDE back end,

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:52.280
is that basically, it speaks the
language server protocol, which

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:54.530
basically, all
editors can speak it,

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:56.597
and the language server
provides these features.

00:19:56.597 --> 00:19:57.930
And that's how they communicate.

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:00.263
So there's a very similar
thing for debuggers, actually.

00:20:00.263 --> 00:20:02.480
It's called DAP, which is
Debug Adapter Protocol.

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:04.280
And so Delve is getting--

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:07.700
basically, one big thing
that the tools team is doing

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:10.970
is working on adding
DAP natively to Delve.

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:14.750
So basically, this is going
to be a debug implementation

00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:18.920
directly in the debugger,
and it's actually--

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:20.900
it's going to be
coming out shortly.

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:23.510
I think it's available right
now in an experimental mode,

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:25.430
but I think it will be
enabled in a preview

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:27.500
version of the extension
by default pretty soon.

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:29.930
And so you can try
that out in VSCode Go.

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:33.120
And what's really nice about
that is it's previously,

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:35.420
this debug adapter had
been written specific

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:39.530
to each editor plug-in, just
like the same thing for the IDE

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:42.440
integrations like gopls solved.

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:44.348
So basically, now,
there's going to be

00:20:44.348 --> 00:20:45.890
a Go implementation
of this protocol,

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:47.737
and any editor can
use it as well.

00:20:47.737 --> 00:20:49.820
So basically, debugging
should become a lot easier

00:20:49.820 --> 00:20:51.050
pretty soon.

00:20:51.050 --> 00:20:52.425
FILIPPO VALSORDA:
That's awesome.

00:20:52.425 --> 00:20:56.570
I knew about the language
protocol, this Language Support

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:58.010
Protocol, is that right?

00:20:58.010 --> 00:20:59.300
What does LSP stand for?

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:01.092
REBECCA STAMBLER:
Language Server Protocol.

00:21:01.092 --> 00:21:03.710
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Language
Server Protocol, yes.

00:21:03.710 --> 00:21:05.308
And I knew about that.

00:21:05.308 --> 00:21:07.850
I didn't know that there was an
equivalent one for debugging.

00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:10.160
And that sounds great,
because, again, that

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:13.010
was so much deprecated
work between each editor

00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:17.090
and IDE having to integrate
with all of the tooling or, even

00:21:17.090 --> 00:21:18.650
worse, rebuild all
of the tooling.

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:20.600
So that's excellent.

00:21:23.685 --> 00:21:27.440
We have another question
from Stephen about fuzzing.

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:30.320
So will fuzzing
be more than just

00:21:30.320 --> 00:21:33.530
type registries with
candidate values per type?

00:21:33.530 --> 00:21:36.080
What kind of smarts
are you fuzzing?

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:38.390
And I think Stephen,
he was probably asking

00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:40.160
about the mutator, I guess?

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:41.090
What do you think?

00:21:41.090 --> 00:21:42.632
KATIE HOCKMAN: Yeah,
I'm actually not

00:21:42.632 --> 00:21:47.072
sure what a type registry
is, but I can definitely

00:21:47.072 --> 00:21:48.780
talk about which types
would be supported

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:50.850
and having [INAUDIBLE]
work in that way.

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:55.220
So right now, in, basically,
the development branch

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:57.350
that we're working in,
it's just supporting

00:21:57.350 --> 00:22:01.570
basic types, like a byte-size
string, an int, a uint,

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:04.230
float, to things like that.

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:07.340
And all those things can
be mutated in the mutator

00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:13.170
and spit out new inputs based
on the values in the corpus.

00:22:13.170 --> 00:22:16.410
And in the future,
we also want to be

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:19.290
able to support more types,
just generally speaking--

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:22.117
like an int slice, for
example, things like that.

00:22:22.117 --> 00:22:23.700
But we also want to
be able to support

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:25.540
struct types in the future.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:27.900
So that doesn't
necessarily mean we're

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:30.850
going to support every
single struct right away,

00:22:30.850 --> 00:22:32.730
but what we can do
at the beginning,

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:36.780
at least, is to just support
structs that basically

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:40.470
have an implementation
of the text

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:44.340
marshalling or binary
marshalling interface.

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:46.890
And so what we can do
is, then, just mutate

00:22:46.890 --> 00:22:49.870
the bytes under the
hood, and then just

00:22:49.870 --> 00:22:51.900
unmarshall it back
into the struct value

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:54.430
before we run it against
the fuzz function.

00:22:54.430 --> 00:22:56.790
And that's something we
hope to have as well.

00:22:56.790 --> 00:23:00.870
And more details on that, as far
as how it works under the hood

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:03.240
and what kinds of things
are going to be supported,

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:06.180
you can see better on the
design draft that's online.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:11.940
That's at
golang.org/s/draft-design--

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:14.850
I think it's
draft-fuzzing-design.

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:18.796
Or you can just Google it; it
should come up pretty quickly.

00:23:18.796 --> 00:23:20.528
FILIPPO VALSORDA:
I hear this Google

00:23:20.528 --> 00:23:21.570
engine works pretty well.

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:28.770
[LAUGHS] Now, we talked about
stability for a second earlier.

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:31.590
What are some
features on pkg.go.dev

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:35.220
that can help me evaluate
if a project is stable?

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:37.270
JULIE QIU: Yeah, that's
a great question.

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:40.020
So on a high level,
on the right-hand side

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:43.170
of every package page, you'll
see this little detail section.

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:46.380
And that will tell you if you're
looking at a stable version

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:47.558
of a package.

00:23:47.558 --> 00:23:49.350
And so if the author
is following Go module

00:23:49.350 --> 00:23:52.350
conventions, and a package
is expected to be stable once

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:56.190
it's been tagged with a
v1.0 or higher release.

00:23:56.190 --> 00:23:57.690
And then, additionally,
if an author

00:23:57.690 --> 00:23:59.430
decides to track
their package version

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:01.565
or the entire module
has been deprecated,

00:24:01.565 --> 00:24:02.940
you'll see a bright
yellow banner

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:05.730
across the site that
tells you, you probably

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:07.780
don't want to use this package.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:09.540
We also have some
features on our roadmap

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:12.270
that are going to help
developers evaluate stability.

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:16.320
So I mentioned earlier about
showing more about the package

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:18.610
API history from
version to version

00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:21.780
so that viewers can see if the
package API is going to change

00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:23.460
across different versions.

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:25.260
We're also planning
to surface information

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:28.060
from the Go
Vulnerability Database,

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:29.550
and lastly, also,
more information

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.830
about whether or not
specific identifiers

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:36.360
are deprecated within
the API itself.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:38.250
FILIPPO VALSORDA: And
we got another question

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:41.140
that I feel like is
paired with this one.

00:24:41.140 --> 00:24:44.550
Why do Go modules require
SemVer, semantic versioning?

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:48.750
Because you mentioned
V0, V1, and maybe

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:52.020
you can say a little more about
what semantic versioning is

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:54.450
and how it plays into APIs?

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:55.770
JULIE QIU: Yeah, definitely.

00:24:55.770 --> 00:24:59.850
Semantic versioning is basically
just a convention that is used,

00:24:59.850 --> 00:25:03.240
not just in Go, but
specifically with Go modules.

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:07.110
It's a way for the author to be
able to indicate the, I guess,

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:10.030
level of stability that you
can expect from this project.

00:25:10.030 --> 00:25:13.860
And so if it's V0,
then it's expected

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:18.000
that the author might be
causing changes in their API.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.130
They might potentially
be deleting an identifier

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:21.780
from version to version.

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:24.240
Effectively, this package
is not stable yet.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:26.220
And because it's
V0, the author is

00:25:26.220 --> 00:25:28.630
following the convention
of indicating to its users,

00:25:28.630 --> 00:25:29.820
this is not stable.

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:32.790
Whereas once you
get to V1 or higher,

00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:35.240
then something that you
want to be able to do

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:36.990
is make sure that from
version to version,

00:25:36.990 --> 00:25:38.680
you're not breaking your users.

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:43.170
So say I'm using a
function foo in 1.5.2.

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:46.060
I don't want to download 1.6,
and then, all of a sudden,

00:25:46.060 --> 00:25:48.120
the author deleted
the identifier,

00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:49.770
and I can't use it anymore.

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:53.610
And that's also why
Go has this convention

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:57.270
of using deprecations and
adding a deprecated comment when

00:25:57.270 --> 00:26:00.090
an author doesn't want to
use an identifier anymore.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:02.400
But it should still
be there in case

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:05.393
a user was depending on that
in the previous version.

00:26:05.393 --> 00:26:06.810
And I think the
last piece of this

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:08.710
is really just major versions.

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:13.195
And so if you have a change
between V1 and V2 and V3,

00:26:13.195 --> 00:26:15.570
it's expected that it's possible
to have breaking changes

00:26:15.570 --> 00:26:17.130
between different
major versions .

00:26:17.130 --> 00:26:19.560
And so Go actually
has semantic import

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:22.620
versioning so that the import
path changes to indicate this

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:24.930
to users as well.

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:26.616
FILIPPO VALSORDA:
Yeah, the point

00:26:26.616 --> 00:26:28.670
about multiple
major versions is, I

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:30.630
think, something that
comes in very handy

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:34.170
when you end up depending
on something that depends--

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:36.990
on two things that depend on
two different major versions

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:38.860
of the same thing.

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:41.910
This is usually called
the diamond problem,

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:46.200
and in Go, that problem
is just solved by the fact

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:49.980
that you can have two
different major versions

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:52.950
of the same module
in the same build.

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:55.410
And if they don't have
the same major version,

00:26:55.410 --> 00:26:56.760
then they need to be compatible.

00:26:56.760 --> 00:26:58.385
And if they're
compatible, you can just

00:26:58.385 --> 00:27:00.130
use the most recent version.

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:02.340
So if two things
depend on V1, we'll

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:04.660
just pick the newest version.

00:27:04.660 --> 00:27:07.470
And if a thing depends
on V1 and one on V2,

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:09.760
we'll just have
them both available.

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:12.120
That's just one of the
design bits of modules

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:14.691
that I always really liked.

00:27:14.691 --> 00:27:17.460
And you also answered part
of one of the questions

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:21.330
that we got earlier that is
about the crypto libraries,

00:27:21.330 --> 00:27:24.480
which is, how often you have
to deprecate older crypto

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:26.850
libraries, and do these
deprecations break

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:28.540
older versions of Go?

00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:32.340
So there are two places where
we have crypto libraries,

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:36.030
and for context, Go comes with
a complete set of cryptography

00:27:36.030 --> 00:27:38.580
libraries, including things
like TLS, and encryption,

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:40.180
and signatures.

00:27:40.180 --> 00:27:45.360
And we do deprecations-- we
did a bunch of deprecations

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:47.850
in the last few years, partially
because the libraries are

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:50.490
almost 10 years old,
and we want to make sure

00:27:50.490 --> 00:27:52.950
that anything that you
find in these libraries

00:27:52.950 --> 00:27:56.280
is something that
you would want to use

00:27:56.280 --> 00:28:00.090
and that is modern
enough that this is not

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:01.980
going to be a [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:04.290
So we do deprecations,
but deprecations are just

00:28:04.290 --> 00:28:05.910
surfaced to tooling.

00:28:05.910 --> 00:28:07.420
They will just, for
example, show up

00:28:07.420 --> 00:28:09.060
in your editor
saying, hey, you're

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:10.620
using this thing
that is deprecated.

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:13.350
And here's some text that
explains the deprecation,

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:15.780
and here's what you
can use instead.

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:18.720
And they absolutely
never break in the code,

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:20.650
whether they are in
the standard library,

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:24.630
or in the GoLang or
[INAUDIBLE] repository.

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:28.690
Because more of them follow
the Go backwards compatibility

00:28:28.690 --> 00:28:31.470
promise, so make
sure that they're

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:33.400
always backwards compatible.

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:35.820
So we use deprecation instead
to communicate about things

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:37.965
that we kind of
wish weren't there,

00:28:37.965 --> 00:28:39.840
but we're going to keep
them there because we

00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:41.880
don't want to break anybody.

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:44.460
[INAUDIBLE]

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:48.990
OK, so our time is,
I think, running out.

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:51.600
So we have one last
question, which

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:54.690
I think everybody can answer.

00:28:54.690 --> 00:28:58.000
What is one thing you would
recommend checking out for Go?

00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.550
Katie, do you want to start?

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:00.925
KATIE HOCKMAN:
Yeah, so one thing

00:29:00.925 --> 00:29:03.810
I think that's worth
checking out is go.dev.

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:07.470
This is something that
Julie could also, probably,

00:29:07.470 --> 00:29:09.130
care a lot about.

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:14.503
But go.dev is going to be
a new way to explore Go.

00:29:14.503 --> 00:29:16.920
In the past, everyone went to
golang.org to read about it,

00:29:16.920 --> 00:29:19.140
but go.dev has a
lot of cool things

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:22.350
like case studies
and information about

00:29:22.350 --> 00:29:23.467
how it can be used.

00:29:23.467 --> 00:29:25.050
And so if you're an
enterprise and you

00:29:25.050 --> 00:29:26.580
want to look into
adding Go, that's

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:28.470
a really good place to start.

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:30.840
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Julie?

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:33.220
JULIE QIU: Yes, go.dev is very
near and dear to my heart,

00:29:33.220 --> 00:29:35.670
so I hope that
everyone checks it out.

00:29:35.670 --> 00:29:37.920
But I think one of the
things that I really

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:39.840
loved about building
the Go Package Discovery

00:29:39.840 --> 00:29:41.640
site is actually
just discovering

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:45.270
some really amazing packages
for my development workflow.

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:48.060
And some that we
use all the time

00:29:48.060 --> 00:29:51.330
is Static Check, which runs
static analysis on your code.

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:54.780
And it's caught tons of bugs and
made improvements to my code.

00:29:54.780 --> 00:29:58.170
Unparam has helped me catch
a ton of unused functional

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:00.700
parameters and
results in my code.

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:03.720
Misspell has helped me catch
a lot of commonly misspelled

00:30:03.720 --> 00:30:04.350
English words.

00:30:04.350 --> 00:30:05.970
And so those are
some packages that I

00:30:05.970 --> 00:30:09.030
really love, and use all time,
and recommend checking out.

00:30:09.030 --> 00:30:11.130
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Yeah,
those are excellent.

00:30:11.130 --> 00:30:11.630
Rebecca?

00:30:11.630 --> 00:30:13.005
REBECCA STAMBLER:
Yeah, for sure,

00:30:13.005 --> 00:30:14.790
Static Check is
available in your editor.

00:30:14.790 --> 00:30:17.040
So definitely use that.

00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:19.950
But I would recommend checking
out the Go Playground, which

00:30:19.950 --> 00:30:22.768
is a sandbox where you can
basically play with Go code

00:30:22.768 --> 00:30:24.810
without having to have
anything on your computer.

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:26.260
And you can have multiple files.

00:30:26.260 --> 00:30:28.980
You can use third party
dependencies in it.

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:29.860
It's really awesome.

00:30:29.860 --> 00:30:31.860
It's a really quick way
to just write something,

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:34.650
if you want, as just
like a scratch project.

00:30:34.650 --> 00:30:36.030
FILIPPO VALSORDA: Wonderful.

00:30:36.030 --> 00:30:39.300
So on my side, something that
I used just a couple of days

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:41.250
ago is this package
called errgroup

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:42.750
that you can find
in the golang.org

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:47.190
at sync module, which allows
you to run in parallel

00:30:47.190 --> 00:30:48.450
a number of Go [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:48.450 --> 00:30:51.720
And just when one of them
hits an error, all of them

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:54.360
get canceled, and the error
gets returned at the end.

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:57.900
I find that just such a nice
way to build parallelization

00:30:57.900 --> 00:30:59.370
into the programs.

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:02.640
Now, this has been
fun, and we didn't

00:31:02.640 --> 00:31:05.100
get to all of the
questions, but we will

00:31:05.100 --> 00:31:06.730
try to answer them in the Dory.

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:08.770
And if anybody has
any other questions,

00:31:08.770 --> 00:31:11.550
you can always reach the Go
community and the Go team

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:14.430
on the golangnuts--
like in peanuts--

00:31:14.430 --> 00:31:17.060
mailing list and on
the Go First Slack.

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:19.770
Thank you, everyone, and have
a great rest of the conference.

